+## User styling
+
+With `launch-tunnel-manager` or `agent_console --static ...`, the page loads an optional
+`~/.config/hpclib/console/user.css` after the bundled `style.css`. If `HPCLIB_CONSOLE_DIR` is set,
+the file belongs in `$HPCLIB_CONSOLE_DIR/user.css` instead. Keep personal styling here so it survives
+updates to hpclib. Create the directory and file yourself; the console does not create or overwrite it.
+
+```bash
+mkdir -p "${HPCLIB_CONSOLE_DIR:-$HOME/.config/hpclib/console}"
+# edit user.css in that directory, then reload the browser page
+```
+
+For example, `user.css` can contain:
+
+```css
+:root { --accent: #7c3aed; }
+@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
+  :root { --accent: #a78bfa; }
+}
+body { font-size: 16px; }
+main { max-width: 1500px; }
+hpc-panel::part(panel) { border-radius: 12px; }
+hpc-dialog::part(dialog) { width: min(640px, calc(100vw - 32px)); }
+```
+
+Use the variables in `style.css` for colours: `--bg`, `--fg`, `--muted`, `--line`, `--panel`,
+`--accent`, `--good`, `--warn` and `--bad`. They also reach the panels' and dialogs' shadow DOM.
+For their internal layout, use the exposed `::part(...)` selectors instead of descendant selectors:
+`hpc-panel` exposes `panel`, `header`, `heading`, `status`, `body` and `close`; `hpc-dialog`
+exposes `dialog` and `heading`. Other elements can be styled with ordinary selectors.
+Normal CSS specificity still applies; later rules win when specificity is equal.
+
+The console serves only this file at `/user.css`, without a session key, just like its bundled static
+assets. An absent file returns an empty stylesheet. It reads the file on each request and sends
+`Cache-Control: no-store`, so edits take effect after a full browser reload (the console's **Refresh**
+button refreshes page data only). The rest of the console directory is not served.
+This automatic loading applies to the page served by `agent_console --static`; a separately hosted
+front end must arrange to serve its own `user.css` at the same URL on its own origin.
+
 ## Components
 
 `components.js` defines the page's building blocks as native custom elements (no library, no build step);
